ties on a wide variety of aspects of the DEBs, given parameter values. The file pars_my_pet in the add_my_pet-collection generates these predictions. The idea is to inspect this list critically and, in the case of unlikely predictions, add a likely value to the list of observations and repeat the parameter estimation procedure till all inconsistencies are resolved, see Appendix A. We hope that this procedure will guide experimental research. We see this check as an essential step in an estimation procedure, where physical, chemical and biological realism has priority over goodness of fit. Where the classic approach in statistics is to assume that the model is true and the observations suffer from noise, we here want to fit a model which we know is very simple, given the immense complexity of living organisms. We know a priori that the model is wrong in the strict sense of the word, but possibly useful. In this paper, we first describe the structure, state variables and parameters of the standard DEB model and their relationship to empirical observations. We then outline the covariation method to estimate parameters from data. In the companion paper, we study the properties of the method and present the beginnings of a parameter library for diverse species and the initial patterns that emerge from it (Lika et al., 2011-this issue). While the present method only exploits the covariation of parameter values implied by physical laws, future developments might also exploit evolutionary and ecological patterns to reduce the need for data and increase efficiency and reliability.
2. Relating variables to observations
To understand how the abstract parameters of the DEB theory can be estimated from observations in the real word, it is necessary to provide a brief summary of the structure of DEB theory and the different types of parameters and variables. The standard DEB model applies to an isomorphic organism (no change in shape during growth). Knowledge of the parameters of the DEB model allows predictions of changes in the state variables of structure, reserve, reproduction buffer and maturity through ontogeny under any combination of food
levels and body temperature. This gives a complete, stoichiometrically constrained picture of fluxes of energy and mass as the organism grows, develops, reproduces and ages throughout its life cycle in dynamic environments (temperature, food availability). The standard DEB model achieves this using a single food type, a single reserve and a single structure, all with a constant chemical composition (although these components may differ in composition from each other).
