2.4. The rules for parameter covariation and their relation to body-size scaling patterns
The basic philosophy of DEB theory is to access the parameters for many individuals of many species independently (in principle) and plot them in the parameter space, giving one point per individual. We expect that the points of individuals that belong to the same species tend to cluster. The covariation rules tell how these parameter values are distributed in this space, based on plain physics only. The reasoning does not include any selective pressures acting independently on parameter values. The covariation rules can thus be considered as a “null expectation” for parameter covariation and their impact on the related physiological quantities. Evolutionary and ecological adaptation can cause deviations from these physical expectations, so these deviations can guide us to specific adaptations. The popular approach of body size scaling relationships might look similar at first sight, but we see important differences with what we try to do. Where body size is treated in such relationships as an independent variable and eco-physiological properties are regressed empirically as (allometric) functions of body size, body size in the context of DEB theory is a result of underlying processes, is not considered as an independent variable and is affected by environmental variables. DEB theory also refrains from empirical relationships and, thus, does not make use of allometric functions. It is then possible to write physiological quantities, such as maximum body weight, minimum incubation time, or maximum reproduction or respiration rate as functions of parameters. These expressions can then be rearranged so that a physiological process, e.g. respiration, is a function of body weight. DEB theory sharply distinguishes between intra-species comparisons (different body sizes, same parameters) and inter-species comparisons (different body sizes but also different parameters). This essential distinction is typically absent in many body size scaling
relationships in the literature. The specific respiration of a small young mouse is higher than that of a fully grown one, because the young one grows. The specific respiration of a fully grown mouse is higher than that of a fully grown elephant, but obviously for a very different reason, which is well-understood in the context of DEB theory (Kooijman, 1986). A large collection of eco-physiological quantities (Kooijman, 1986, 2010) was found to be highly consistent with empirical interspecies body size scaling patterns. Although observed physiological quantities might depend on maximum body weight in rather complex ways, the covariation structure of the primary parameters is very simple. The rules for the covariation of parameter values, which we exploit in the present parameter estimation method, result from a combination of three conditions:
1. all parameters have a clear physical meaning and can, therefore, be classified as intensive or design parameters 2. appropriate ratios of design parameters are intensive (in view of their physical meaning) 3. maximum structural length depends on a single design parameter (so no degree of freedom to link parameter values to length).
The first condition classifies DEB theory as mechanistic by definition. The last condition ensures that the maximum specific assimilation rate, p ̇ Am
f g, is proportional to the maximum structural length. The second condition links all other parameters to each other. Notice that the rules for the covariation of parameters follow from core theory and are therefore fully independent of auxiliary theory and of empirical observations. These rules are implemented in the parameter estimation procedure using the concept of pseudo-data described above, in that only intensive parameters can play the role of pseudo-data points. The covariation rules can also be useful for making inter-specific comparisons of parameter values because it allows all organisms to be normalised to a common maximum (structural) body length. The comparison is done on the basis of a reference maximum structural body length of Lrmef = 1 cm using the zoom factor z = Lm/Lrmef, where
