4. Dealing with data quality
4.1. Weight coefficients
When estimating DEB parameters with the covariation method on the basis of diverse data sets, one frequently faces the issue that certain observations have been made with greater confidence or accuracy than others. It is therefore useful to be able to assign relative weights to the different data points on the basis of this prior knowledge, which is done by the weight coefficients. If predictions match observations perfectly, the choice of weight coefficients is irrelevant, i.e. different (non-trivial) choices lead to exactly the same parameter estimates. The importance of the weight coefficient increases with the deviations between predictions and observations, so with our unhappiness with the end result. Weight coefficients can still be used in the ln likelihood function for the multiple data sets. The ML estimates in the case of the constant variation coefficient are typically close to the weighted least squares (WLS) criterion for the estimation of parameters, if these weight coefficient are chosen inversely proportional to the squared observations, both between and within data sets. These weight coefficients not only account for differences in units of the various data-entries (deviations with different units cannot be added), but also for the accuracy (or certainty if you like) of the individual data points. Subsequently factors are applied to increase or decrease the importance of data in determining the estimated parameter values, accounting for presumed accuracy. Pseudo-data typically get lower weight coefficients relative to real data. The role of the weight coefficients in the resulting estimates increases with the variance. In absence of deviations between observations and predictions, weight coefficients are irrelevant, and the ML and the WLS criteria then lead to the same result. If the scatter is not small, the difference between the results of both criteria can be substantial, but they become uninspiring anyway. The reasons for the large deviations should be understood first otherwise the parameter estimates are useless. The importance of the estimation criterion should not be overestimated in practice. The set of pseudo-data consists of the set of typical values of potentially all primary parameters for a generalised animal that do not relate to the zoom factor as indicated in Table 1. Since these parameter values are conceived as data, the estimated parameter values can (and do) deviate from the values of the pseudo-data, depending on the choice for the weight coefficients. A high weight coefficient for a particular pseudo-data point corresponds with a small variance of the prior distribution for the corresponding parameter of the Bayesian method.
4.2. Assessing the completeness of real data
The covariation method we have described here means that DEB parameters can be assessed with a wide variety of different types of data. While this greatly increases the scope for DEB parameter estimation, it also results in a wide range in the underlying quality of data on which the parameter estimates are based. Thus, when reporting the estimated parameters for the DEB model, it is useful to have a means to assess the relative quality of different real data sets. The completeness of real data can be classified naturally in 10 levels (Table 3). Since it is impossible to obtain energy balances without having mass balances, or to obtain mass balances without having composition data, a natural sequence in the availability level exists, which is reflected in Table 3. These levels are rather qualitative in nature, and well-studied species have completeness levels around 3 or 4. This low level is primarily due to the fact that most data in the literature has been obtained by workers who had concepts in mind that differ substantially from those used by DEB theory. Moreover quite a few fluxes are very difficult to measure and very few species allow culturing under carefully controlled conditions; their life cycle covers
a period that substantially exceeds that of a typical research project. It is almost impossible to quantify feeding (including food availability, quality and selection) for animals living in the field. Lack of data will always be a basic fact of life and sets an upper limit for the complexity (parameter richness) of applicable models. Thanks to the rules for covariation of parameter values, the standard DEB model is unique in its capacity to be relatively parameter-rich and still applicable as demonstrated in the add_my_pet data-collection (see Appendix A). The standard DEB model assumes isomorphy for structure, i.e. the shape (of structure) does not change during growth. Most types of measures and measurements speak for themselves, but auxiliary theory for the standard DEB model needs to be subtle for length measures. Three types of length-measures are delineated:
1. Physical length Lw is the actual length of a body, defined for a particular shape. 2. Physical volumetric length is the cubic root of physical volume (and does not depend on shape). 3. Structural length L is the volumetric length of structure, where the individual is assumed to consist of structure, reserve and the reproduction buffer. This length cannot be measured directly, only indirectly.
An assumption of auxiliary theory for the standard DEB model is that the shape coefficient δM 1⁄4 L=Lw is assumed to be constant for a well-chosen length measure. For humans, for instance, the head-tofeet length hardly depends on the amount of reserve, but the belt length does. Physical volume is typically larger than structural volume to accommodate reserve, but in some taxa (e.g. most ecdysozoa), reserve is replaced by water during starvation.
4.3. Pseudo-data considerations
The pseudo-data consists of typical values for a generalised animal, as listed in parameter Tables 1 and 2. These values are based on large collection of estimated parameters from various data sets from a wide variety of species, as presented in Kooijman (2010) and in Cardoso et al. (2006), Jager et al. (2004) and Veer et al. (2006, 2010). Yet some parameters have been estimated more frequently than others (because low real data completeness levels suffice) and in many cases compound parameters could be estimated, where primary parameter could not. Although these compound parameters do not provide information on any single primary parameter, they do restrict possible variation in the set of primary parameters. The resulting parameter combination was subsequently tested and adjusted
