5. Discussion and summary
We have presented a new approach of parameter estimation, the covariation method, that permits all parameters of the standard DEB
model to be estimated simultaneously from a collection of common empirical datasets. Apart from the real data, the estimation method makes use of pseudo-data, exploiting the rules for the covariation of parameter values among species that are implied by the standard DEB model. The general idea behind the covariation method is to let all available information compete (interact if you like) to produce the end result. We are then independent of any particular sequence in estimating parameter values. To see why this single-step procedure is important, consider the following example. The Arrhenius temperature TA affects rates; the observations contain some rates (e.g. the reproduction rate R ̇ i at temperature T) and the pseudo-data contain some rates (e.g. the energy conductance v ̇ at temperature Tref). The prediction of  ̇Ri depends on v ̇ . The simultaneous minimization of the deviation of the predictions for R ̇ i and  ̇v forces TA at a particular value. The following helps to see why. With TA completely free we can set the deviation from the pseudo-data-point v ̇ equal to zero (estimate for  ̇v equals the pseudodata-point), without providing any constraint on the prediction for R ̇ i, because it is at another temperature. With a two-step procedure in which we first estimate TA from one data set, we prescribe TA in the second step and do not let the information on TA in the first step compete with that in the second step. By fixing TA in the second step, the minimization of the deviation from the pseudo-data-point  ̇v translates into a constraint for the minimization of the deviation from  ̇Ri. The general idea of the method is that the least information that has to be supplied is the maximum length, and all parameter values follow from the pseudo-data as a kind of minimum requirement. Using this method, we do not suffer from the problem that the combination of real and pseudo-data does not have enough information to estimate the set of all parameters, that is the core parameters, the shape coefficient and the temperature parameters. The composition parameters are typically treated as given, or the values of the generalised animal are used; notice that these parameters control respiration, in combination with some core-parameters. Unless sufficient real data are available, only the Arrhenius temperature is estimated among the temperature parameters. If only maximum size is known, all parameters can still be estimated, but the pseudo-data fully control the result. Increasing the number of types of real data (so information) decreases the role of the pseudo-data in the parameter estimation. Ideally so much data is available that the pseudo-data hardly play a role. Common practice is typically somewhere between these extremes, and our motivation is to serve practice. As mentioned in the Introduction, the philosophy behind this approach is that parameter estimation in the context of DEB theory is more than just giving an accurate description of the data; the parameters should also make physiological, physical and chemical sense. For this purpose the resulting estimates should not deviate too much from the parameter values for the generalised animal, since animals share most metabolic properties and machinery. If unknown, the maturity thresholds at birth and puberty can be added to the pseudo-data if coupled to the zoom factor (in fact the maximum specific assimilation rate  ̇pAm
f g), but they show in practice so much more scatter (high evolutionary adaptability) that is this hardly realistic. This also means that these thresholds contribute considerably to position the species in the diversity spectrum. Observed size and/or age at birth and puberty must be present in the data to approach realism, and the use of pseudo-data for these parameters is better avoided (which corresponds with giving these pseudo-data points weight coefficient zero). Working with the hidden variables of the standard DEB model (structure, reserve, maturity) might be inconvenient. However, we hope we have demonstrated that it is in no respect problematic. The solution to the problem of hidden variables is the simultaneous estimation of parameters for a set of various observations on feeding, growth, reproduction, etc. In this way it is possible to quantify the amount of reserve and structure of an individual and to access their
