chemical composition, for instance, without being able to measure this directly. All quantitative questions about them (such as the fraction of body weight that relates to reserve, and the composition of reserve) can be answered, using relatively simple measurements on growth, reproduction and composition of biomass. The present pillar of auxiliary theory for the standard DEB model to access the amount of structure is a well-chosen physical length as proxy for structural length; lengths play a key role in DEB theory because somatic maintenance is linked to volume and uptake to surface area, so their ratio is linked to length for isomorphs. To what extent reserve and structure remain hidden variables in the future is still an open question. The amounts of DNA and ribosomal RNA have been proposed as proxies for structure and reserve, respectively, on the basis of their concentration as function of the growth rate (Kooijman, 2010, p 143). How well these proxies behave under starvation conditions is presently unknown due to lack of data. Since the concentrations of DNA and rRNA differ between organs and tissues, their amounts must be measured for a whole organism and it must be homogenised first. This is not only destructive, but might also be problematic for large body sizes. To what extent DNA and rRNA concentrations in a particular tissue can be used as proxies is unknown; if this would work, it might be possible to measure them in a non-destructive way. Effects of toxicants can greatly help to determine the value of (all) parameters; in quite a few situations toxicants modify only a single parameter in a low range of internal concentrations. The successful use of specific toxicants to quantify parameter values not only helps to determine these values, but also strongly supports the structure of the DEB model (Baas et al., 2010a,b; Jager and Klok, 2010; Jager et al., 2004, 2006, 2010). This also points to a fundamental problem: environmental conditions other than food availability and quality and temperature, can affect parameter values (think e.g. of acidity, alkalinity, humidity, diurnal and seasonal cycles). If the measured data refer to a variety of environmental conditions, accuracy might be lost due to variation of parameter values (e.g. linked to the geographical distribution of the species). Data availability is typically a problem, let alone when we constrain it to the same environmental conditions. Moreover organisms can switch conditions, sometimes as a function of their life cycle. We need parameter-rich models to capture the wide variety in performances of organisms, but parameter-poor ones to avoid the excessive need for data. We believe that the standard DEB model represents a useful compromise between these conflicting needs.
