Appendix A. Data and parameter collection add_my_pet
We applied the estimation method in practice on data from a wide variety of species, which can be found at http://www.bio.vu.nl/thb/ deb/deblab/add_my_pet. Each species has 3 files for use in Matlab or Octave in combination with software package DEBtool:
• the mydata-file, which specifies the observations and measurements, sets the initial parameter values for estimation, runs the estimation procedure using software package http://www.bio.vu.nl/ thb/deb/deblab/debtool and presents the results. • the predict-file, which calculates the predicted values for the measurements on the basis of the standard DEB model, given the parameter values. • the pars-file, which runs DEBtool-scripts to show a list of over 100 ‘implied properties’.
The idea behind the pars-file is not only to predict unobserved quantities, but also to check if all implied properties seem realistic. If contra-evidence is available, the next step is to include this evidence in the mydata-file and re-run the estimation procedure. The mydata-files show marks for completeness to the data, according to the levels delineated in Table 3 and the goodness of fit of the real data. Routines DEBtool/lib/regr/mre or DEBtool/lib/regr/mrevc can be used for this purpose for the WLS and ML criteria, respectively. If the type of data exactly matches an existing mydata-file, e.g. mydata_my_pet, no further coding is required and the corresponding predict_file can be used. One only needs to replace the data to generate the required predictions.
